                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "234344943",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198052832628",
                            "num_reviews": 8,
                            "playtime_forever": 2480,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 2464,
                            "last_played": 1789680339
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "KAMUI is basically Taito's Rayforce, but combined with ChoRenSha 68k and other similar early bullet hell titles - faster-paced, more manic and with more, game-altering resources to manage. The big gimmick that distuinguishes it from either of its predecessors is the huge frickin' laser beam that you can use both as an offensive and as a defensive tool. Don't go in thinking that you'll 1CC the game in your first try just because of this almighty bullet-wiping beast of a weapon, though: using it slows you down, it's on a cooldown timer that seems incredibly short at first but WILL bite you in the ass in later stages, and has an incredibly narrow area of effect that extends to the bottom of your ship but not its sides, so you better learn how to dodge and misdirect enemy shots if you want to survive the game's five stages. The boss battles are KAMUI's highlights and the major progression walls you will face when striving for a 1CC, requiring a fair amount of memorization and skillful dodging of thick, but not screen-filling bullet patterns through classic shmup maneuvers (streaming, micrododging, short misdirection) if one wants to emerge unscathed from them - which is essential for survival due to how stingy the game is with HP recovery item drops, despite first impressions (there are no life extends available either!).\r\n\r\nPresentation-wise, KAMUI is gorgeous for 1999 doujinsoft standards - certainly more impressive than the first few Windows Touhou games, which, may I remind you, started coming out a whole three years after this game's release -, but all of its graphical tricks and setpieces are pulled almost directly from Rayforce, with the exception of the boss battles - which is one more reason for why they are so incredibly memorable. The soundtrack however is wholly original, opting for a more cinematic flair akin to Radiant Silvergun and Sokyuugurentai's (within the limitations of fairly standard MIDI instruments), and very very memorable.\r\n\r\nOverall, KAMUI gets a STRONG RECCOMMEND from the STG Skunk!\r\n\r\nPersonal Challenge Level: Medium-Hard\r\nTime dedicated: 40 hrs\r\nWinning Run Score: 12,432,070\r\nPersonal Best Score: 13,795,640",
